    "essay": "\nAURELIUS AUGUSTINUS, known to the Church as Saint Augustine of Hippo, Doctor of Grace, was born at Thagaste in Numidia (modern Souk Ahras, Algeria) on 13 November 354, and died at Hippo Regius on 28 August 430. His mother, Saint Monica, was a Christian of rare perseverance; his father, Patricius, received baptism only late in life. The outlines of his education, conversion, and episcopate are known with unusual clarity from his own *Confessiones*, from the *Vita* by Possidius, and from the vast correspondence and sermons that survive.\n\nAugustine was trained in rhetoric at Madaura and Carthage. For nearly a decade he adhered to Manichaeism, seeking in its dualism an explanation of evil. Disillusioned, he taught rhetoric at Carthage, Rome, and Milan. Under the preaching of Saint Ambrose and the reading of the Platonists, his intellectual difficulties concerning the nature of God and of evil were gradually removed; the moral crisis of his conversion is dated to 386 in the garden at Milan (*Conf.* VIII). Baptized by Ambrose at the Easter vigil of 387, he returned to Africa, founded a monastic community at Thagaste, was ordained priest at Hippo in 391, and consecrated coadjutor bishop about 395, succeeding to the full care of the see shortly thereafter.\n\nAs bishop he preached almost daily, administered a busy African diocese, and composed the works that fixed the direction of Latin theology. Against the Donatists he defended the unity and holiness of the Church independent of the personal worthiness of her ministers, and the validity of baptism conferred outside full communion when conferred with the right form and intention. Against the Pelagians—Pelagius, Caelestius, and later Julian of Eclanum—he insisted that the fallen will cannot attain justification by exhortation alone; that infants need baptism for the remission of original sin; and that grace is prevenient, gratuitous, and efficacious without destroying true freedom. The Second Council of Orange (529) later confirmed the main anti-Pelagian line of this teaching for the Western Church.\n\nHis principal works include the *Confessiones*; *De civitate Dei*, written in the shadow of Alaric’s sack of Rome (410); *De Trinitate*; the anti-Pelagian treatises (*De peccatorum meritis*, *De spiritu et littera*, *De natura et gratia*, *De gratia et libero arbitrio*, *De correptione et gratia*, *De praedestinatione sanctorum*); the *Enarrationes in Psalmos*; and numerous tractates on the Gospel of John. Philosophically he transmitted a Christianized Platonism; theologically he ordered the Latin vocabulary of sin, grace, predestination, and the Church. The Rule associated with his name shaped later canons and the Augustinian friars.\n\nAugustine’s central concern may be stated as the restless will’s true end in God. Desire, for him, is not first a vice but the movement of a soul made for the Good; sin is disordered love; evil is privation rather than a positive substance. Grace heals and elevates freedom rather than replacing it with mechanism. The Church is the body of Christ in history, mixed in her members until the final judgment, yet one in her sacraments and apostolic faith.\n\nHe was declared a Doctor of the Church by popular acclamation long before formal lists; his feast is kept on 28 August. Few Western theologians have equalled his influence. Aquinas, Luther, Calvin, Jansen, and Newman all read him—often against one another—yet the Catholic Church continues to receive him as the Doctor of Grace within the rule of faith defined by Orange, Trent, and the ordinary magisterium. Primary orientation for the student remains the *Confessiones*, *De civitate Dei*, and the mature anti-Pelagian writings, read with the canons of Orange and the Tridentine decree on justification.\n",
