    "essay": "St. Thomas Aquinas (1225–1274), St. Dominican friar, Doctor of the Church, Doctor Angelicus — Doctor Angelicus, whose synthesis of Aristotelian philosophy and Christian revelation shaped Catholic theology for centuries..\n\nLife. SAINT THOMAS AQUINAS, Dominican friar, priest, and Doctor of the Church (Doctor Angelicus), was born at Roccasecca in the Kingdom of Sicily about 1225 and died at Fossanova on 7 March 1274 while journeying to the Second Council of Lyons. Of noble lineage, he entered the Order of Preachers against family opposition, studied under Saint Albert the Great at Cologne and Paris, and taught at Paris, Orvieto, Rome, and Naples. He was canonized by John XXII in 1323 and declared a Doctor of the Church by Pius V; Leo XIII in *Aeterni Patris* (1879) commended his philosophy as a sure guide for Christian schools.\n\nTeaching. Thomas’s literary output is immense: the *Scriptum* on the Sentences; the *Summa contra Gentiles*; the unfinished *Summa theologiae*; extensive Aristotelian commentaries; biblical commentaries (notably on Job, the Pauline epistles, and the Gospel of John); the *De unitate intellectus* against Averroism; the *Office* of Corpus Christi; and numerous disputed questions (*De veritate*, *De potentia*, *De malo*, *De spiritualibus creaturis*).\n\nHis method is the scholastic *quaestio*: objections, *sed contra*, corpus, replies. Beneath the method lies a theological architecture often summarized as *exitus–reditus*—creatures proceed from God and return to God through Christ. God is *ipsum esse subsistens*, not a being among beings; creatures exist by participation. The five ways (*Summa theologiae* I, q. 2, a. 3) argue from motion, efficient causality, contingency, degrees of perfection, and finality to a First Principle whom all call God. Faith and reason cannot ultimately conflict; grace perfects nature rather than destroying it.\n\nIn Christology and sacramentology Thomas is decisive for later Catholic doctrine. The hypostatic union, the fittingness of the Incarnation, the Passion as satisfaction and sacrifice, and the conversion of the Eucharistic substance (*transubstantiatio*) receive classic formulation. Habitual grace, the infused virtues, and the gifts of the Holy Spirit structure the moral and mystical life. Natural law is the rational creature’s participation in the eternal law.\n\nPlace in the Church. The tradition names him Angelic Doctor for the purity and elevation of his teaching on the angels and on sacred doctrine. Students approach him best through the *Summa theologiae* (especially I, I-II, III on Christ and the sacraments) and the *Contra Gentiles*, with the awareness that he is a commentator on Scripture and the Fathers as well as a philosopher. His feast is 28 January (formerly 7 March).",
