    "essay": "St. Ignatius of Antioch (c. 35–c. 107), Bishop of Antioch, Apostolic Father, Martyr — Bishop of Antioch, Apostolic Father.\n\nLife. St. Ignatius of Antioch, also called Theophorus (\"God-bearer\"), was bishop of the Church in Syria and suffered martyrdom at Rome under Trajan about the year 107. While being conveyed as a prisoner from the East to the capital, he wrote letters to the churches of Ephesus, Magnesia, Tralles, Rome, Philadelphia, and Smyrna, and to Polycarp of Smyrna. The seven letters of the so-called middle recension are accepted by the great majority of modern scholars as authentic. After the New Testament they form one of the purest and most urgent witnesses to early Catholic structure and belief.\n\nTeaching. Ignatius insists on the unity of the local church under one bishop, with presbyters and deacons—an early and clear testimony to the threefold ministry. He exhorts the faithful to do nothing without the bishop and to gather for the one Eucharist. Of that sacrament he writes with unmistakable realism: the bread is the flesh of Jesus Christ, who suffered for our sins and whom the Father raised up. Against Docetism he argues that if Christ's suffering was only appearance, then Ignatius's own chains and coming death are a deception. The Incarnation and the Cross are the measure of every later claim about salvation.\n\nHis desire for martyrdom is not a hatred of life but a form of discipleship. \"I am the wheat of God,\" he writes to the Romans, \"and I am ground by the teeth of wild beasts, that I may be found pure bread of Christ.\" The letters should be read entire: they are brief, urgent, and free of the later technical vocabulary of the councils, yet they already speak the language of eucharistic realism, episcopal unity, and the true flesh of the Savior.\n\nHis feast is kept on 17 October in the present General Roman Calendar. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts; the judgment of the Church in liturgy and doctrine; and sober historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. The doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.\n\nPlace in the Church. In this archive the present article is an orientation, not a substitute for the sources. Where Ignatius has left writings, begin there. Measure every later mysticism and every private claim by the common faith, the commandments, and the unity of the Church under her bishops.",
    "name": "St. Ignatius of Antioch",
    "centralQuestion": "How does faithful suffering in union with Christ make the Church's unity visible?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His Central Question",
