        "idea": "Christ is the Logos in whom all truth finds its source and fulfillment.",
        "source": "First Apology 46; Second Apology 10–13",
        "article": [
          "Justin Martyr, philosopher and convert, teaches that the divine Logos—the Word who became incarnate in Jesus Christ—is the source of every fragmentary truth the world has known. The seeds of the Word (sperma tou Logou) are scattered among the nations; whatever philosophers and poets said truly, they said by participation in that Logos. In Christ the seed becomes the full plant. This is not a baptism of every pagan opinion; it is a claim that truth has one source and one fulfillment.",
          "The doctrine serves mission and judgment together. Mission: the apologist can honor what is true in Greek wisdom without treating the Gospel as a barbarian novelty. Judgment: philosophies that contradict the incarnate Word are not autonomous peaks but incomplete or distorted participations. Catholic engagement with non-Christian thought still moves in this Justinian pattern when it is faithful—affirm, purify, fulfill—rather than either pure rejection or pure relativism.",
          "Justin’s Logos Christology is also firmly incarnational. The Word is not a distant principle; He was made flesh, suffered, and rose. The martyrs die for a Person, not for an abstraction. Later Alexandrian and Cappadocian theology will refine the vocabulary of Logos and hypostasis; Justin already refuses to let the philosophical title float free of the crucified Jesus.",
          "There is a pastoral edge for an age of competing worldviews. The Catholic need not pretend that outside the Church there is only darkness; nor may he treat every insight as a parallel revelation equal to the Gospel. Christ is the criterion. Truths find their home in Him; errors are exposed by Him.",
          "Read the Apologies as a method: patient argument before the empire, confidence that the Logos has not left Himself without witness, and clarity that baptism into Christ is not optional for those who have recognized the fullness. Philosophy can be a tutor; it is not the bridegroom."
        ],
