    "essay": "St. Justin Martyr (c. 100–c. 165), Philosopher, Apologist, Martyr — Philosopher and Apologist.\n\nLife. St. Justin Martyr (c. 100–c. 165) was a philosopher who became a Christian apologist and ultimately a martyr at Rome. Born in Flavia Neapolis in Samaria, he searched among the philosophical schools for a truth that could claim the whole person. He found in Christianity not the negation of philosophy's genuine desire for truth, but its fulfillment in the Logos who had become flesh.\n\nTeaching. His First and Second Apologies address the Roman authorities with a double claim: Christians are not atheists or criminals, and the faith can give a reasoned account of itself. Justin is among the first to describe Christian worship in plain terms for outsiders, including the baptismal initiation and the Eucharist. Of the latter he writes that the food over which thanksgiving has been spoken is not common bread and drink but the flesh and blood of the incarnate Jesus.\n\nIn the Dialogue with Trypho he engages Jewish objection and develops a reading of the Old Testament as preparation for Christ. The same Logos who ordered creation has been at work in the prophets; the Scriptures are not discarded but fulfilled. This is an early form of the Christian claim that revelation has a history and a center—the Incarnation—without becoming a mere allegory that dissolves the letter.\n\nJustin's Logos doctrine would later require the precision of the councils. He sometimes speaks in ways that later orthodoxy would tighten. Yet the trajectory is clear: the divine Reason through whom all things were made is the One who was born, suffered, and rose. Philosophy's best questions are not silenced; they are answered in a Person.\n\nPlace in the Church. He was martyred under the prefect Rusticus. The Acts of his martyrdom show a teacher who will not sacrifice to the gods. For this archive, Justin is the figure of the converted mind: the restless search that ends not in skepticism but in worship, baptism, and the altar.",
