    "essay": "St. Irenaeus of Lyons (c. 130–c. 202), Bishop of Lyons, Theologian — Bishop of Lyons, opponent of Gnosticism.\n\nLife. St. Irenaeus of Lyons (c. 130–c. 202) is the great anti-Gnostic theologian of the second century and one of the principal architects of the Catholic rule of faith. Born in Asia Minor and formed in the tradition that remembered Polycarp, he became bishop of Lyons in Gaul. His major work, Against Heresies (Adversus Haereses), systematically refutes the Valentinian and related systems that opposed the Creator to the Redeemer and treated the material world as a mistake.\n\nTeaching. Irenaeus's central claim is simple and immense: the same God who created the world is the God who redeems it in Jesus Christ. There is one economy of salvation, not a secret higher god behind the inferior demiurge. The Word becomes what we are so that we may receive participation in what belongs to God. Salvation therefore restores and elevates created human nature; it does not abolish the body or despise history.\n\nAgainst private, elitist revelations, Irenaeus appeals to the public succession of bishops in the churches founded by the apostles, especially the Church of Rome. Apostolic succession is not a mere bureaucracy; it is a criterion of teaching. The rule of faith—the unified pattern of creation, Incarnation, passion, resurrection, and the Spirit—is confessed openly in baptism and liturgy.\n\nHis doctrine of recapitulation (anakephalaiosis) gathers the whole story of Adam into Christ. What was fractured in the first man is summed up and healed in the second. Mary's obedience is set in parallel with Eve's disobedience—not as a rival gospel, but as a sign that the Incarnation reaches the whole human drama.\n\nPlace in the Church. Irenaeus remains a doctor of unity: one God, one Christ, one faith, one Church. The later councils will speak with technical precision about person and nature; they walk a road he already marked against the divided worlds of the Gnostics.",
    "name": "St. Irenaeus of Lyons",
    "centralQuestion": "How does the incarnate Word recapitulate and heal the whole of human history?",
