    "essay": "Tertullian (c. 155–c. 220), Apologist, Theologian, Montanist (later) — Father of Latin theology.\n\nLife. Tertullian of Carthage (c. 160–c. 225) is the first major Christian theologian to write in Latin and one of the sharpest minds of the pre-Nicene Church. A trained rhetorician, he turned his gifts to the defense of the faith in works such as the Apology, the Prescription Against Heretics, On the Flesh of Christ, On the Resurrection of the Flesh, and Against Praxeas. His legacy is double: he forged much of the Latin theological vocabulary, and later in life he fell into the rigorism of the Montanist movement—an error the Church does not follow even while she continues to read his orthodox writings with profit.\n\nTeaching. Against heresies that treat the rule of faith as optional, Tertullian argues that the Church possesses a public tradition against which novel interpretations must be measured. The Prescription is a legal metaphor: heretics have no standing to argue from Scripture until they accept the Church's title to the Scriptures. The move is controversial in method; the instinct is Catholic—Scripture is the Church's book, not a quarry for private systems.\n\nOn the Flesh of Christ and On the Resurrection of the Flesh defend the realism of the Incarnation and the bodily resurrection against Docetic and dualist dilutions. If Christ's flesh is appearance, redemption is appearance. If the resurrection is only of the soul, the Gospel has been half-denied. Tertullian's polemical heat sometimes overstates; his target is clear: salvation concerns the whole human being.\n\nAgainst Praxeas is a foundational Latin text on the Trinity. Tertullian speaks of one substance and three persons (una substantia, tres personae), language that would echo for centuries. He fights both the division of the monarchy of God and the confusion of the persons. Precision about unity and distinction is already, for him, a requirement of Christian speech about God.\n\nPlace in the Church. The Catholic reader receives Tertullian with discernment. Where he defends the rule of faith, the flesh of Christ, the resurrection, and the triune mystery, he is a giant of the tradition. Where rigorism and Montanism claim him, the Church does not follow. The greatest ideas listed below are drawn from the permanent deposit of his orthodox contribution.",
    "name": "Tertullian",
    "centralQuestion": "How can the Church preserve the rule of faith against innovation without becoming merely reactionary?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His Central Question",
