          "Athanasius treats the body assumed by the Word as the instrument through which human nature is healed and divinized. The flesh is not a disguise or a temporary tool discarded after use; it is the permanent means of the economy. What is not assumed is not healed—Gregory Nazianzen will state the axiom sharply; Athanasius already lives it in his account of the Incarnation. The Word uses a real body so that real bodies may be raised and real human nature may be restored.",
          "This instrumental christology is ordered to the resurrection and to the sacraments. The same body that dies and rises becomes the source of life for those who are joined to him. Eucharistic realism and the hope of the resurrection of the flesh both stand in an Athanasian climate: matter is not the enemy of salvation; matter is taken up into the Word’s work.",
          "Against dualist contempt for the body and against a purely spiritualized gospel, Athanasius holds the body’s dignity as the Word’s instrument. Pastoral care of the sick, reverence for the dead, and the blessing of ordinary embodied life all find a theological root here.",
