    "essay": "St. John Chrysostom (c. 347–407), Archbishop of Constantinople, Doctor of the Church, Preacher — Archbishop of Constantinople, “Golden-Mouthed”.\n\nLife. St. John Chrysostom (c. 349–407), bishop of Constantinople and Doctor of the Church, is the supreme preacher of the Greek Fathers. Called “Golden Mouth” for the power of his homilies, he left an immense corpus on Matthew, John, Acts, the Pauline epistles, and the priesthood, together with the series On the Statues preached in crisis at Antioch. His theology is pastoral: doctrine becomes formation of a people capable of living the Gospel.\n\nTeaching. Chrysostom refuses to separate the altar from the poor. The Eucharist and care for the suffering belong to one charity; to honor the Body on the altar while neglecting the body of the poor is a contradiction he will not allow. Christ is encountered not only in the consecrated gifts but in the hungry, the naked, and the imprisoned—Matthew 25 as a present rule of recognition. Preaching is not performance; it is the formation of conscience and community. Repentance is medicinal transformation rather than mere legal penalty. On the Priesthood remains a classic: the priest must cultivate an interior life capable of carrying the burdens of others. Office without holiness is a danger; eloquence without love is noise.\n\nPlace in the Church. His own life—reform of the court and clergy, conflict with empress and officials, final exile—shows the cost of that standard. In this archive the Catholic preacher and confessor still drinks from his homilies. Read On the Priesthood and the homilies on Matthew concerning the poor. The measure of the sermon is still the same: did the people become more capable of the Gospel’s life?",
    "name": "St. John Chrysostom",
    "centralQuestion": "How does the Word preached form a moral and eucharistic people in the city?",
