        "idea": "Scripture should be approached through its original languages rather than through secondary versions alone.",
        "source": "Prefaces to the Vulgate; letters",
        "article": [
          "Jerome’s life work of translating the Scriptures from Hebrew and Greek into Latin rests on a single conviction: the Church should hear the Word as closely as possible to the languages in which it was given. Secondary versions are useful; they are not a substitute for the sources. Catholic biblical scholarship’s permanent debt to the Vulgate begins in this severity toward secondhand hearing. The Hebraica veritas—the Hebrew truth—is not a rejection of the Septuagint’s authority in the Church’s life, but a demand that the translator and the teacher not rest content with a chain of intermediaries when the originals can be studied.",
          "Jerome’s prefaces and letters show the cost of the project: controversy with those who preferred the familiar Latin of the Old Latin versions, the labor of learning Hebrew, the defense of decisions that startled liturgical habit. The saint of Bethlehem is not only an ascetic; he is a philologist of the sacred page. Learning becomes a form of obedience to the Word that refuses to stay at a comfortable distance.",
          "The Church later received the Vulgate as her ordinary Latin Bible for centuries. Modern Catholic biblical studies, with their insistence on original languages in the formation of clergy and scholars, stand in continuity with Jerome’s instinct even when they use tools he did not have. The point is not antiquarianism; it is reverence that takes the trouble to hear accurately.",
          "Study the Scriptures in the original languages when vocation and capacity allow. Prefer Jerome’s severity toward secondhand hearing to pure dependence on a single vernacular without remainder. Let the labor of the languages be a form of prayer and service.",
          "Scripture should be approached through its original languages rather than through secondary versions alone. Jerome’s permanent gift is this philological reverence—the Bethlehem scholar’s proof that love of the Word includes the hard work of hearing it as nearly as possible as it was first spoken and written."
        ],
