          "Jerome’s lifelong labor on the biblical text is offered as a form of Christian service—to the Latin Church, to the preachers who will need a reliable Bible, to the faithful who will hear the Word in the liturgy. Catholic intellectual apostolate finds in him a patron of scholarship as charity. The desk in Bethlehem is an apostolic station. Manuscripts, languages, and prefaces are not a private hobby; they are a work of mercy for the people who will never learn Hebrew but will live by the book he produces.",
          "This doctrine dignifies the hidden labor of translators, editors, teachers, and students of Scripture in every age. Not every Christian is called to Jerome’s philology; every Church needs some who are. The service is measured by fidelity to the text and usefulness to the body, not by the scholar’s fame.",
          "If scholarship is your vocation, offer it as service as Jerome did. Prefer the labor that feeds the Church’s hearing to pure academic display. Let the Vulgate’s long use still define success—a book that served a people’s prayer for centuries.",
