    "essay": "St. Jerome (c. 347–420), Priest, Doctor of the Church, Biblical translator — Translator of the Vulgate, Doctor of the Church.\n\nLife. St. Jerome (c. 347–420), Doctor of the Church, is the supreme biblical scholar of the ancient Latin Church. From his labors came the Vulgate—the Latin translation that would form the Western mind for a millennium—and a mass of commentaries, letters, and polemical works. Irascible, learned, and ascetic, he made the original languages of Scripture a duty of Christian service rather than a luxury of specialists.\n\nTeaching. Jerome’s program is clear: Scripture should be approached through Hebrew and Greek rather than through translation alone. Returning to the sources can illuminate the Church’s understanding; the translator is a servant of the text, not its master. His prefaces and letters defend this labor against those who preferred a familiar Latin without checking the wells from which it was drawn. Controversies with Augustine about the LXX and the Hebrew show a living debate; both remain doctors of the Church. Ascetic discipline, for Jerome, should serve holiness rather than become a substitute for charity—though his rhetoric often needs the Church’s balancing voice. Scriptural scholarship itself is a form of Christian service: hours over a difficult verse are hours given to those who will hear the verse proclaimed.\n\nPlace in the Church. The Catholic reader receives Jerome as the doctor of the sacred page, while noting that his polemical heat sometimes wounded friends and opponents alike. In this archive Jerome is the patron of the desk as apostolate. Read the prefaces to the Vulgate and the letters on translation. Fidelity to the Word includes the humility of philology under the Church’s rule of faith. Anti-intellectual suspicion of language study cannot claim him as patron.",
