    "essay": "St. Cyril of Alexandria (c. 376–444), Patriarch of Alexandria, Doctor of the Church — Patriarch of Alexandria, champion of the Theotokos.\n\nLife. St. Cyril of Alexandria (c. 376–444), patriarch and Doctor of the Church, is the decisive theological voice of the Council of Ephesus (431) and the great defender of the unity of Christ’s person against Nestorian division. His letters to Nestorius, the Twelve Chapters, and the treatise That Christ Is One articulate the confession that the one born of Mary is the divine Word incarnate—and that Mary is therefore rightly called Theotokos, Mother of God.\n\nTeaching. Cyril’s central claim is that Christ is one Person, not a partnership between two persons. The humanity remains genuine; the acting subject is the eternal Word. All the acts of Christ—birth, suffering, rising—are acts of the one Word in His own flesh. The Incarnation makes salvation possible because God truly enters human existence. The Eucharist communicates the life of the incarnate Word because the flesh belongs to the one divine Person—hence Cyril’s strong realism about the sacrament. The controversies of Ephesus were bitter, and Cyril’s political methods have been judged severely by historians. The Catholic Church receives his doctrine as a pillar of orthodoxy.\n\nPlace in the Church. Chalcedon (451) would balance Cyril’s unity with a clearer dyophysite vocabulary; it did not reverse his core confession. In this archive Cyril is the doctor of the one Christ and of the Mother of God. Read the letters to Nestorius and On the Unity of Christ. The title Mother of God is soteriological before it is devotional: if Mary is only mother of a human person joined to God, the Gospel of the Incarnation is already divided.",
    "name": "St. Cyril of Alexandria",
    "centralQuestion": "How can the Word who is truly God be truly born of Mary for the salvation of human nature?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His Central Question",
