    "essay": "SAINT LEO I, surnamed the Great, pope from 29 September 440 until 10 November 461, Doctor of the Church, was a Roman deacon of established authority before his election and became the outstanding Western doctrinal voice of the mid-fifth century.\n\nLife. Leo governed the church of Rome during the disintegration of Western imperial security. He faced the doctrinal crisis associated with Eutyches and the Robber Council of Ephesus (449), the threat of Attila in 452, and the Vandal sack under Gaiseric in 455. Whether every detail of the embassies is recoverable, the tradition rightly remembers a bishop who held together doctrine, discipline, and the civic welfare of Rome when civil power failed. He strengthened the pastoral oversight of the Roman see over the Western churches and insisted on the reception of the faith of Chalcedon.\n\nThe Tome and the Council of Chalcedon. Letter 28 to Flavian of Constantinople, known as the Tome, written in 449 against Eutyches, affirms that one and the same Christ is consubstantial with the Father according to divinity and consubstantial with us according to humanity, the properties of each nature being preserved and coming together in one Person. At the Council of Chalcedon in 451 the Tome was received with the acclamation that Peter had spoken through Leo. Subsequent letters of Leo pressed the definition upon sees that hesitated or resisted.\n\nSermons and letters. Leo’s sermons on the Nativity, the Epiphany, the Passion, the Ascension, and Pentecost display classical Latin structure and a theology ordered to the liturgy: what the Church celebrates, the bishop explains. The correspondence deals with appeals, clerical discipline, and the dignity of the Roman see as servant of the faith of the whole Church rather than as a substitute for the councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. The feast of Saint Leo the Great is kept on 10 November. Primary reading includes the Tome (Letter 28) and Sermons 21 to 30 on the Nativity cycle. Leo remains the measure of Latin clarity on the Incarnation between the Council of Ephesus and the later Christological schisms. His prose continues to model episcopal teaching that is both precise and pastoral.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nHow to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
    "name": "St. Leo the Great",
    "centralQuestion": "How does the one person of Christ in two natures secure both the truth of the Incarnation and the hope of salvation?",
