    "essay": "SAINT ANSELM OF CANTERBURY, archbishop, Benedictine, and Doctor of the Church, 1033–1109, father of scholasticism.\n\nLife. Monk and abbot of Bec, archbishop of Canterbury in conflict with William Rufus and Henry I over the liberty of the Church, twice exiled.\n\nWritings and teaching. Monologion; Proslogion; Cur Deus homo; treatises on the Trinity, original sin, and freedom.\n\nDoctrine. Faith seeking understanding; the argument from that than which nothing greater can be thought; satisfaction theory of the Atonement.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 21 April. The student should begin with the following: the Proslogion and Cur Deus homo. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nHow to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.\n\nAnselm of Canterbury (c. 1033–1109), Benedictine abbot and archbishop, stands among the chief architects of the Latin theological method. His Proslogion is not a mere puzzle about a definition of God; it is a prayer in which the mind seeks to understand what faith already holds: that God is that than which nothing greater can be thought. The Monologion and the treatises on truth, free will, and the fall of the devil show the same pattern — fides quaerens intellectum.\n\n\"I do not seek to understand in order that I may believe, but I believe in order to understand.\" — St. Anselm, Proslogion, 1\n\nAgainst the background of feudal imagery, Cur Deus Homo asked why God became man. Anselm’s satisfaction account of the Atonement would shape Western soteriology for centuries, even where later theologians revised his categories. He wrote as a monk and a bishop under political pressure, not as a lecturer in a modern faculty.\n\n\"The satisfaction must be made by a God-man, because only man owes it and only God can pay it.\" — Summary of Cur Deus Homo’s central claim, in Anselm’s own structure\n\nThe Church receives Anselm as a doctor of the intellect at prayer. His method is neither rationalism nor fideism: faith seeks understanding, and understanding returns to praise. In the encyclopedia of Catholic thought he marks the turn from the early medieval school to the high scholastic confidence that reason, under faith, can serve the mysteries without mastering them.",
    "name": "St. Anselm of Canterbury",
    "centralQuestion": "How can reason, seeking understanding of faith, show why God became human for our salvation?",
