    "essay": "SAINT BONAVENTURE, Franciscan, cardinal, and Doctor of the Church (Seraphic Doctor), c. 1217–1274, minister general of the Friars Minor.\n\nLife. Born at Bagnoregio, he governed the order through crises of poverty and growth, wrote the official Life of Saint Francis, and died at the Second Council of Lyons.\n\nWritings and teaching. Itinerarium mentis in Deum; Breviloquium; commentary on the Sentences; disputed questions.\n\nDoctrine. Illumination of the mind; ascent from vestige to image to excessus of contemplation; Franciscan wisdom ordered to love.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 15 July. The student should begin with the following: the Itinerarium entire. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nHow to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.\n\nBonaventure (c. 1217–1274), Franciscan minister general and doctor of the Church, ordered the Franciscan inheritance into a theological itinerary. The Itinerarium mentis in Deum, the Breviloquium, and the Legenda maior of St. Francis show a mind at once scholastic and affective.\n\n\"Christ is the way and the door; Christ is the ladder and the vehicle.\" — St. Bonaventure, Itinerarium mentis in Deum, 7\n\nAgainst a purely Aristotelian confidence, Bonaventure insisted that the light of faith and the illumination of the Word remain necessary for the mind’s journey into God. Creation is a ladder of vestiges; the soul is the image; the goal is ecstatic love beyond the limits of discursive reason.\n\n\"If you ask how these things can occur, ask grace, not instruction; desire, not understanding; the groan of prayer, not diligence in reading.\" — St. Bonaventure, Itinerarium, 7, 6\n\nThe Catholic Church numbers him among the seraphic doctors. His work remains a bridge between Francis’s poverty and the university’s questions, without allowing either to abolish the other.",
    "name": "St. Bonaventure",
    "centralQuestion": "How does the mind's journey into God pass through creation, the soul, and grace without leaving the crucified Christ?",
