    "essay": "St. Teresa of Ávila (1515–1582), Carmelite reformer, Mystic, Doctor of the Church — Carmelite mystic, Doctor of the Church.\n\nLife. SAINT TERESA OF ÁVILA (Teresa of Jesus), virgin, reformer of Carmel, and Doctor of the Church, 1515–1582, first woman Doctor of the Church (1970). St. Teresa of Ávila (1515–1582) defined mental prayer as “intimate sharing between friends… taking time frequently to be alone with Him who we know loves us” (Life / Way of Perfection tradition; English translations in the public domain for older editions). The Interior Castle maps growth under ordinary conditions: self-knowledge, struggle, detachment, and union as gift. Dryness tests whether the soul loves the Giver or the gifts. She subjects experiences to confessors and the Church’s rule of faith. The Carmelite reform is the institutional form of the teaching: prayer under a rule, with ordinary work.\n\nTeaching. Life. Born at Ávila, she entered the Incarnation monastery, underwent conversion to serious mental prayer, founded Saint Joseph’s in 1562, and with John of the Cross extended the Discalced reform to the friars. Canonized 1622.\n\nWritings and teaching. Life; Way of Perfection; Interior Castle; Foundations; letters. She maps the grades of prayer from meditation to spiritual marriage with psychological precision and theological sobriety.\n\nDoctrine. Infused contemplation as gift not technique; necessity of the humanity of Christ, growth in virtue, and obedience; reform of community life as protection of prayer.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 15 October. The student should begin with the following: Way of Perfection and Interior Castle, mansions I, IV, and VII. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
