    "essay": "St. John of the Cross (1542–1591), Carmelite, Mystic, Doctor of the Church — Carmelite mystic, Doctor of the Church.\n\nLife. SAINT JOHN OF THE CROSS, Discalced Carmelite and Doctor of the Church, 1542–1591, collaborator of Teresa of Ávila in the reform. St. John of the Cross (1542–1591) described the active and passive nights that strip sensory and spiritual attachments so that faith, hope, and love remain. Older English translations of the Ascent and Dark Night are in the public domain. The path of nada is ordered to the todo that is God. Detachment is not stoic emptiness; it is liberty for the Beloved. Union is transformation in love, not annihilation of the person. The dark night is distinguished from ordinary melancholy by its fruits: humility, love, and service. Directors are to test spirits.\n\nTeaching. Life. Born at Fontiveros, he joined the Carmelites, worked with Teresa, was imprisoned at Toledo by opponents of the reform, and wrote his greatest poetry in captivity. Canonized 1726; Doctor 1926.\n\nWritings and teaching. Ascent of Mount Carmel; Dark Night; Spiritual Canticle; Living Flame of Love—prose commentaries on poems of the same names. The doctrine of nada, the active and passive purifications of sense and spirit.\n\nDoctrine. Infused contemplation; the dark night as God’s purifying work; union in the theological virtues and the Cross under strict orthodoxy.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 14 December. The student should begin with the following: the poems and the Dark Night. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
