    "essay": "St. John Henry Newman (1801–1890), Cardinal, Theologian, Convert — Cardinal, theorist of the development of doctrine.\n\nLife. SAINT JOHN HENRY NEWMAN, cardinal of the Holy Roman Church, 1801–1890, leader of the Oxford Movement and convert of 1845. St. John Henry Newman (1801–1890) traced the development of doctrine and the grammar of assent. His major works are not all public domain in every edition; the archive uses careful short quotation from PD-status texts where available and clear paraphrase where not. Development: authentic growth preserves type, continuity of principles, and power of assimilation—corruptions break the type. The illative sense names how real assent is reached in concrete reasoning. He is kept as a modern doctor of conscience and development within the Catholic reception.\n\nTeaching. Life. Educated at Oxford, vicar of Saint Mary’s, he entered the Catholic Church on 9 October 1845, founded the English Oratory, served the Catholic University of Ireland, and was created cardinal by Leo XIII in 1879. Canonized 2019.\n\nWritings and teaching. Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine; Apologia pro vita sua; Grammar of Assent; Idea of a University; sermons; Letter to the Duke of Norfolk; Dream of Gerontius.\n\nDoctrine. Legitimate development of doctrine; real assent and the illative sense; conscience under the magisterium against private judgment and religious liberalism.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 9 October. The student should begin with the following: the Apologia and the conclusion of the Essay on Development. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
