    "essay": "St. Bernard of Clairvaux (1090–1153), Cistercian abbot, Doctor of the Church — Mellifluous Doctor, reformer and contemplative.\n\nLife. SAINT BERNARD OF CLAIRVAUX, Cistercian abbot and Doctor of the Church (Doctor Mellifluus), 1090–1153, founder of Clairvaux and counsellor of popes. St. Bernard of Clairvaux (1090–1153) entered Cîteaux and founded Clairvaux, becoming the great voice of the Cistercian reform. His On Loving God and Sermons on the Song of Songs map the ascent of charity and the soul’s search for the Word. He advised popes, opposed Abelard’s excesses, and preached the Second Crusade. Contemplation and public action were, for him, one charity ordered to the Bridegroom’s honor in His Bride. “The reason for loving God is God; the measure is to love without measure.” The degrees of love culminate in loving God for His own sake.\n\nTeaching. Life. He entered Cîteaux with a large company, founded Clairvaux in 1115, advised Eugene III, contested Abelard at Sens, opposed the antipope Anacletus, and preached the Second Crusade at Vézelay in 1146. His correspondence shows a European range of influence rare even among medieval abbots.\n\nWritings and teaching. Sermons on the Song of Songs; On Loving God; On Consideration; treatises on humility and on grace and free will; several hundred letters. His Latin is classical yet affective, the classic of twelfth-century monastic theology.\n\nDoctrine. Christocentric bridal mysticism ordered by orthodoxy; love of God in degrees culminating in loving God for God’s sake; monastic poverty and sincerity.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 20 August. The student should begin with the following: On Loving God and Sermons 1–8 on the Song of Songs. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
