          "Augustinian theology in the scholastic age can speak the language of Aristotle without abandoning Augustine’s grace-centered vision. Giles of Rome’s permanent gift is this bilingual fidelity—the doctor’s proof that the prior gift and the Church’s unity can still be argued in the university’s terms, under an order that never sold the center for the tools."
