    "essay": "St. Thomas of Villanova (1488–1555), Augustinian friar, Archbishop of Valencia, Father of the Poor — Augustinian archbishop whose almsgiving and reform earned the title Father of the Poor..\n\nLife. St. Thomas of Villanova, 1488–1555, Augustinian friar, Archbishop of Valencia, Father of the Poor. This figure belongs to the Catholic Reform · Spanish Golden Age. Known in the tradition as “Augustinian archbishop whose almsgiving and reform earned the title Father of the Poor..” Tomás de Villanueva was born at Fuenllana in 1488, studied at Alcalá, and entered the Augustinians. He became a renowned preacher and provincial, known for refusing honors until obedience forced them upon him. As archbishop of Valencia (from 1544) he lived frugally, visited the poor, and organized systematic relief. Contemporaries said he died with little of his own because he had given the rest away. He was beatified in 1618 and canonized in 1658. Spanish art—Zurbarán above all—fixed his image as the bishop who empties his chest for the beggar at the door.\n\nTeaching. Tomás de Villanueva was born at Fuenllana in 1488, studied at Alcalá, and entered the Augustinians. He became a renowned preacher and provincial, known for refusing honors until obedience forced them upon him. As archbishop of Valencia (from 1544) he lived frugally, visited the poor, and organized systematic relief. Contemporaries said he died with little of his own because he had given the rest away. He was beatified in 1618 and canonized in 1658. Spanish art—Zurbarán above all—fixed his image as the bishop who empties his chest for the beggar at the door.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does Augustinian interior poverty become concrete mercy toward the poor? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Evangelical poverty; Episcopal charity; Catholic Reform; Augustinian common life. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Thomas of Villanova is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Thomas of Villanova",
    "centralQuestion": "How does Augustinian interior poverty become concrete mercy toward the poor?",
