    "essay": "St. Rita of Cascia (1381–1457), Wife and mother, Augustinian nun, Patron of impossible causes — Augustinian of Cascia, marked by the thorn of Christ’s Passion and invoked in desperate cases..\n\nLife. St. Rita of Cascia, 1381–1457, Wife and mother, Augustinian nun, Patron of impossible causes. This figure belongs to the Late Medieval Augustinian holiness. Known in the tradition as “Augustinian of Cascia, marked by the thorn of Christ’s Passion and invoked in desperate cases..” Rita was born at Roccaporena near Cascia in 1381. Married young, she endured a turbulent marriage and the murder of her husband in a local feud. She worked for reconciliation between families and eventually saw her sons spared a life of revenge. Widowed, she sought entry among the Augustinian nuns of Cascia; after initial refusal she was admitted and lived a hidden life of prayer, penance, and charity until her death in 1457. Canonized in 1900, she remains one of the most beloved Augustinian saints. Pilgrims still come to Cascia asking her intercession in marriages, illnesses, and causes that seem beyond hope.\n\nTeaching. Rita was born at Roccaporena near Cascia in 1381. Married young, she endured a turbulent marriage and the murder of her husband in a local feud. She worked for reconciliation between families and eventually saw her sons spared a life of revenge. Widowed, she sought entry among the Augustinian nuns of Cascia; after initial refusal she was admitted and lived a hidden life of prayer, penance, and charity until her death in 1457. Canonized in 1900, she remains one of the most beloved Augustinian saints. Pilgrims still come to Cascia asking her intercession in marriages, illnesses, and causes that seem beyond hope.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does patient love under impossible wounds become a path of union with the Crucified? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Forgiveness; Conformity to the Passion; Augustinian Rule; Intercession. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Rita of Cascia is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Rita of Cascia",
