    "essay": "St. Scholastica (c. 480–c. 543), Abbess, Sister of St. Benedict, Patron of Benedictine nuns — Sister of St. Benedict, whose last conversation with him Gregory recounts as a triumph of charity over rule-bound haste..\n\nLife. St. Scholastica, c. 480–c. 543, Abbess, Sister of St. Benedict, Patron of Benedictine nuns. This figure belongs to the Early Western monasticism. Known in the tradition as “Sister of St. Benedict, whose last conversation with him Gregory recounts as a triumph of charity over rule-bound haste..” Scholastica, according to tradition, was Benedict’s twin sister and consecrated to God from early life. She led a community of nuns near Monte Cassino. Gregory the Great’s Dialogues tell of their yearly meeting and of her death around 543, three days after their final conversation. She is the patron of Benedictine nuns; her feast is celebrated on 10 February.\n\nTeaching. Scholastica, according to tradition, was Benedict’s twin sister and consecrated to God from early life. She led a community of nuns near Monte Cassino. Gregory the Great’s Dialogues tell of their yearly meeting and of her death around 543, three days after their final conversation. She is the patron of Benedictine nuns; her feast is celebrated on 10 February.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does monastic charity between brother and sister image the primacy of love over rigid schedule? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Monastic charity; Holy conversation; Benedictine women. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Scholastica is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Scholastica",
    "centralQuestion": "How does monastic charity between brother and sister image the primacy of love over rigid schedule?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "Her Central Question",
