    "essay": "SAINT BEDE the Venerable, priest, monk of Wearmouth-Jarrow, and Doctor of the Church, was born in Northumbria about 673 and died at Jarrow on 25 May 735. Given as a child to the monastery founded by Benedict Biscop, he was ordained deacon at nineteen and priest at thirty by John of Beverley, and spent almost his entire life within the twin monasteries of Wearmouth and Jarrow, occupied in the Divine Office, in teaching, and in writing.\n\nLife and character. In a short autobiographical notice at the end of the Ecclesiastical History, Bede lists his works and records his delight in learning, teaching, and writing amid the monastic observance. The letter of Cuthbert describing Bede’s last days shows him still translating the Gospel of John and excerpts from Isidore, and dying with the Gloria Patri on his lips. He stands for the best of early English monastic culture: loyal to the Roman settlement after Whitby, scholarly without restlessness, and pastoral in the homilies addressed to a mixed audience of monks and laity.\n\nWritings. The Historia ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum, completed in 731, is the foundation of English church history. It narrates the conversion of the Anglo-Saxons, the missions of Augustine of Canterbury and of the Irish monks, the conflict of Celtic and Roman customs, and the age of Theodore of Tarsus, Wilfrid, Aidan, and Cuthbert. Bede also composed extensive biblical commentaries on Genesis, Samuel and Kings, the Gospels, Acts, the Catholic Epistles, and the Apocalypse; homilies for the liturgical year; the De temporum ratione, crucial for medieval chronology and the calculation of Easter; De natura rerum; lives of the abbots of Wearmouth-Jarrow; and a life of Cuthbert in prose and verse. His Latin is lucid, and his handling of sources is careful for the standards of his age.\n\nDoctrine and method. Bede is an Augustinian in theology and a grammarian in exposition. He prefers to transmit the teaching of the Fathers rather than to invent a new system. His chronological work fixed the general use of the Dionysian era in the Latin West. As a historian he subordinates narrative to the glory of God and to the unity of the Church under the see of Peter, without erasing the genuine holiness of Celtic saints.\n\nPlace in the Church. Leo XIII declared Bede a Doctor of the Church in 1899. Dante places him among the theologians of the heaven of the sun in the Paradiso. His feast is kept on 25 May. The student should begin with the Ecclesiastical History, especially Books I to III, and with selected Gospel homilies. Bede remains the type of the scholar-saint for whom research, liturgy, and holiness are not competing lives but one vocation in the cloister.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nHow to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
