    "essay": "St. Francis of Assisi (1181/82–1226), Founder, Stigmatist, Reformer of evangelical life — Il Poverello, Founder of the Friars Minor.\n\nLife. St. Francis of Assisi (1181/82–1226), the Poverello, refocused the medieval Church on evangelical poverty, peace, and the joy of creaturely praise. Son of a merchant of Assisi, he embraced radical dependence on God, gathered brothers into a forma vitae confirmed by Innocent III, and received the stigmata at La Verna. The early Rules, the Testament, the Canticle of the Creatures, and the first lives by Thomas of Celano and Bonaventure are the primary doors into his spirit.\n\nTeaching. Francis is not an enemy of the Church’s order; he is its reformer by embodiment. He obeys the pope, reveres priests, and insists on Catholic orthodoxy even while his poverty judges clerical and lay wealth. Creation is not a rival god but a chorus of brothers and sisters praising the Most High—sun, moon, water, fire, and sister death. The Cross is the center: the stigmata seal a life configured to the poor and crucified Christ. Joy, in the Franciscan sense, is not denial of suffering but the freedom of those who have ceased to clutch—perfect joy even in rejection, according to the familiar story, because charity remains.\n\nLater Franciscan theology—Bonaventure, Scotus—will speculative unfold what Francis lived. The Spiritual Franciscan conflicts after his death show how hard it is to institutionalize radical poverty without rupture. His Testament still points to obedience and Catholic faith as non-negotiable. In this archive Francis is the founder of the Friars Minor and a permanent examination of conscience for every comfortable Church: evangelical radicalism inside unity, or radicalism that breaks the body.\n\nPlace in the Church. Read the Canticle and the Testament before the legends. The poetry is real because the Cross is real; the animals are not the point—praise and poverty are. Every reform movement after him is measured by this: does it remain inside communion while recovering the Gospel’s edge?",
