    "essay": "St. Angela of Foligno (1248–1309), Franciscan tertiary, Mystic, Teacher of the theologians — Franciscan penitent and mystic whose Book of Divine Consolation charts the way of the Cross..\n\nLife. St. Angela of Foligno, 1248–1309, Franciscan tertiary, Mystic, Teacher of the theologians. This figure belongs to the Later Franciscan mysticism. Known in the tradition as “Franciscan penitent and mystic whose Book of Divine Consolation charts the way of the Cross..” Angela was born in Foligno in 1248. After a conversion in adulthood she embraced Franciscan penance, was widowed, and lived in close association with the friars. Her Memorial (or Book) records a series of steps and visions focused on Christ’s Passion. She died in 1309. Franciscans and later spiritual writers prized her as a guide of interior purification; she was canonized by Pope Francis in 2013.\n\nTeaching. Angela was born in Foligno in 1248. After a conversion in adulthood she embraced Franciscan penance, was widowed, and lived in close association with the friars. Her Memorial (or Book) records a series of steps and visions focused on Christ’s Passion. She died in 1309. Franciscans and later spiritual writers prized her as a guide of interior purification; she was canonized by Pope Francis in 2013.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does the Passion of Christ become the path of mystical transformation for a penitent soul? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Passion mysticism; Penitential path; Franciscan tertiary life; Mystical darkness. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Angela of Foligno is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Angela of Foligno",
    "centralQuestion": "How does the Passion of Christ become the path of mystical transformation for a penitent soul?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "Her Central Question",
