    "essay": "St. Thérèse of Lisieux (1873–1897), Discalced Carmelite, Doctor of the Church, Patroness of missions — The Little Flower, Doctor of the Church, whose Little Way teaches trust and love in small things..\n\nLife. SAINT THÉRÈSE OF LISIEUX, Discalced Carmelite and Doctor of the Church, 1873–1897, teacher of the little way. Thérèse Martin was born in Alençon in 1873, entered the Lisieux Carmel at fifteen, and lived a hidden life of prayer until her death from tuberculosis in 1897. Her autobiography, written under obedience, spread rapidly after her death. Pius XI beatified and canonized her; John Paul II declared her a Doctor of the Church in 1997. She remains one of the most beloved modern saints—proof that doctrinal depth can speak in the language of ordinary love.\n\nTeaching. Life. Born Thérèse Martin at Alençon, she entered the Carmel of Lisieux at fifteen and died of tuberculosis at twenty-four. Story of a Soul disclosed her doctrine of spiritual childhood. Canonized 1925; Doctor 1997; patroness of missions.\n\nWritings and teaching. Story of a Soul; letters; last conversations; poems and prayers.\n\nDoctrine. Confidence in merciful love; holiness in ordinary duties; offering for priests and sinners; radical trust rather than extraordinary deeds.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 1 October. The student should begin with the following: Story of a Soul (Manuscripts A–C). In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
