    "essay": "St. Peter Faber (1506–1546), First companion of Ignatius, Priest of spiritual conversation, Reformer by gentleness — The first priest among Ignatius’s companions, master of mild and effective spiritual direction..\n\nLife. St. Peter Faber, 1506–1546, First companion of Ignatius, Priest of spiritual conversation, Reformer by gentleness. This figure belongs to the Founding generation of the Society of Jesus. Known in the tradition as “The first priest among Ignatius’s companions, master of mild and effective spiritual direction..” Pierre Favre was born in Savoy in 1506, roomed with Francis Xavier at Paris, and was the first of the companions ordained. He helped shape the early Society’s practice of the Exercises. He worked in Italy, Germany, and Spain, and died in Rome in 1546 on the eve of being sent to Trent. Beatified in 1872 and canonized by Pope Francis in 2013, he stands for a Jesuitism of listening and interior reform.\n\nTeaching. Pierre Favre was born in Savoy in 1506, roomed with Francis Xavier at Paris, and was the first of the companions ordained. He helped shape the early Society’s practice of the Exercises. He worked in Italy, Germany, and Spain, and died in Rome in 1546 on the eve of being sent to Trent. Beatified in 1872 and canonized by Pope Francis in 2013, he stands for a Jesuitism of listening and interior reform.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does gentle spiritual conversation reform souls without crushing them? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Spiritual conversation; Discernment; Gentle reform; Memoriale. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Peter Faber is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
