    "essay": "SAINT CATHERINE OF SIENA, virgin, Dominican tertiary, and Doctor of the Church, 1347–1380, co-patroness of Italy and of Europe.\n\nLife. Born at Siena, she lived as a Mantellata in prayer and penance, then entered public life: nursing the plague-stricken, reconciling factions, and writing to Gregory XI to urge the return of the papacy from Avignon to Rome. She supported Urban VI in the early Western Schism and died in Rome offering her life for the Church’s unity. Canonized 1461; Doctor of the Church 1970.\n\nWritings and teaching. The Dialogue (Book of Divine Providence) treats discernment, obedience, and reform. Over three hundred letters and a collection of prayers complete the corpus. Her images of Christ the bridge, the cell of self-knowledge, and the blood of Christ as mercy entered the common language of Catholic spiritual theology.\n\nDoctrine. Mystical experience ordered to ecclesial obedience; reform of clergy and laity without separation from the visible Church; inseparable truth and charity.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 29 April. The student should begin with the following: the Dialogue on discretion and the letters to Gregory XI. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nHow to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
    "name": "St. Catherine of Siena",
