    "essay": "St. Vincent Ferrer (1350–1419), Dominican friar, Apostolic preacher, Missionary of conversion — Valencian Dominican whose preaching missions stirred mass conversion and repentance across Western Europe..\n\nLife. St. Vincent Ferrer, 1350–1419, Dominican friar, Apostolic preacher, Missionary of conversion. This figure belongs to the Western Schism · Late medieval mission. Known in the tradition as “Valencian Dominican whose preaching missions stirred mass conversion and repentance across Western Europe..” Vincent Ferrer was born in Valencia in 1350, entered the Order of Preachers, and became one of the most famous preachers of the later Middle Ages. He conducted extensive missions in France, Spain, and beyond, and died in Vannes in 1419. He was canonized in 1455. Art often shows him with the trumpet of the last judgment—an icon of his urgent Dominican word.\n\nTeaching. Vincent Ferrer was born in Valencia in 1350, entered the Order of Preachers, and became one of the most famous preachers of the later Middle Ages. He conducted extensive missions in France, Spain, and beyond, and died in Vannes in 1419. He was canonized in 1455. Art often shows him with the trumpet of the last judgment—an icon of his urgent Dominican word.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does Dominican preaching call a late-medieval world to conversion under the shadow of judgment? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Apostolic preaching; Conversion and penance; Last things; Dominican mission. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Vincent Ferrer is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Vincent Ferrer",
    "centralQuestion": "How does Dominican preaching call a late-medieval world to conversion under the shadow of judgment?",
