    "essay": "St. Martin de Porres (1579–1639), Dominican lay brother, Servant of the poor, Patron of mixed-race peoples — Dominican brother of Lima whose humility and care for the sick made him a universal figure of charity..\n\nLife. St. Martin de Porres, 1579–1639, Dominican lay brother, Servant of the poor, Patron of mixed-race peoples. This figure belongs to the Colonial Lima · Baroque holiness. Known in the tradition as “Dominican brother of Lima whose humility and care for the sick made him a universal figure of charity..” Martín de Porres Velázquez was born in Lima in 1579. He entered the Dominican convent of the Holy Rosary as a lay brother and spent his life in humble offices and care of the poor. He died in 1639, was beatified in 1837, and canonized by John XXIII in 1962. He is patron of mixed-race people, public health workers, and those seeking interracial justice—always as a Dominican of charity.\n\nTeaching. Martín de Porres Velázquez was born in Lima in 1579. He entered the Dominican convent of the Holy Rosary as a lay brother and spent his life in humble offices and care of the poor. He died in 1639, was beatified in 1837, and canonized by John XXIII in 1962. He is patron of mixed-race people, public health workers, and those seeking interracial justice—always as a Dominican of charity.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does Dominican charity cross the barriers of race and class in ordinary service? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Humble service; Charity across barriers; Dominican lay brotherhood; Care of the sick. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Martin de Porres is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Martin de Porres",
