    "essay": "St. Hildegard of Bingen (1098–1179), Benedictine abbess, Doctor of the Church, Visionary and composer — Sibyl of the Rhine, Doctor of the Church, abbess of vision and song..\n\nLife. SAINT HILDEGARD OF BINGEN, abbess and Doctor of the Church, 1098–1179, visionary, composer, and teacher. Hildegard was a Benedictine abbess in the Rhineland, writer of visionary books, medical texts, and a large body of liturgical song. Benedict XVI declared her a Doctor of the Church in 2012.\n\nTeaching. Life. Enclosed as a child with Jutta of Sponheim, she later governed Rupertsberg and Eibingen, recorded visions with ecclesiastical approval, composed liturgical music, wrote on medicine and nature, and corresponded with popes and princes. Declared Doctor by Benedict XVI in 2012.\n\nWritings and teaching. Scivias; Liber vitae meritorum; Liber divinorum operum; Symphonia; Physica; Causae et curae; letters.\n\nDoctrine. Theology of living light; virginal Church; cosmic praise; virtue and vice in dramatic combat; integration of music, doctrine, and cosmology.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 17 September. The student should begin with the following: selections from Scivias and the musical compositions of the Symphonia. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
    "name": "St. Hildegard of Bingen",
    "centralQuestion": "How do visionary knowledge, music, and cosmology serve the living Light in a Benedictine key?",
