    "essay": "St. Mary Magdalene de Pazzi (1566–1607), Carmelite mystic, Lover of the Eucharist, Teacher of pure love — Florentine Carmelite of intense Eucharistic and reparative mysticism..\n\nLife. St. Mary Magdalene de Pazzi, 1566–1607, Carmelite mystic, Lover of the Eucharist, Teacher of pure love. This figure belongs to the Florentine Carmel. Known in the tradition as “Florentine Carmelite of intense Eucharistic and reparative mysticism..” Caterina de’ Pazzi entered the Carmel of Florence and took the name Mary Magdalene. She died in 1607 after a life of unusual mystical intensity. Canonized in 1669, she remains a Carmelite witness to Eucharistic love.\n\nTeaching. Caterina de’ Pazzi entered the Carmel of Florence and took the name Mary Magdalene. She died in 1607 after a life of unusual mystical intensity. Canonized in 1669, she remains a Carmelite witness to Eucharistic love.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does Carmelite love of the Eucharist become a life of mystical reparative charity? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Eucharistic mysticism; Reparation; Pure love. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Mary Magdalene de Pazzi is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
    "name": "St. Mary Magdalene de Pazzi",
    "centralQuestion": "How does Carmelite love of the Eucharist become a life of mystical reparative charity?",
