    "essay": "St. Colette of Corbie (1381–1447), Poor Clare reformer, Foundress of Colettine houses, Ascetic — Reformer of the Poor Clares who restored strict poverty and enclosure..\n\nLife. St. Colette of Corbie, 1381–1447, Poor Clare reformer, Foundress of Colettine houses, Ascetic. This figure belongs to the Late medieval Franciscan reform. Known in the tradition as “Reformer of the Poor Clares who restored strict poverty and enclosure..” Colette of Corbie reformed the Poor Clares in the early fifteenth century, establishing the Colettine observance. She died in 1447 and was canonized in 1807.\n\nTeaching. Colette of Corbie reformed the Poor Clares in the early fifteenth century, establishing the Colettine observance. She died in 1447 and was canonized in 1807.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How can the Poor Clare life be restored to the first rigor of Franciscan poverty? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Poor Clare reform; Gospel poverty; Enclosure. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Colette of Corbie is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
