    "essay": "St. Maximilian Kolbe (1894–1941), Conventual Franciscan, Apostle of the Immaculata, Martyr of charity — Franciscan who volunteered to die in place of a fellow prisoner at Auschwitz..\n\nLife. SAINT MAXIMILIAN KOLBE, Conventual Franciscan and martyr of charity, 1894–1941, volunteer victim at Auschwitz. Maximilian Kolbe was a Conventual Franciscan priest, missionary publisher, and founder of the Militia of the Immaculata. He died at Auschwitz in 1941 after taking another prisoner’s place. Canonized in 1982.\n\nTeaching. Life. Born Raymond Kolbe, he founded the Militia of the Immaculata and major publishing apostolates in Poland and Japan. Arrested by the Gestapo, he offered his life for a fellow prisoner at Auschwitz and died on 14 August 1941. Canonized 1982.\n\nWritings and teaching. Articles and conferences on Marian consecration and the Immaculata; spiritual notes of the Militia.\n\nDoctrine. Total consecration to Mary as path to Christ; charity unto death; Franciscan identity under totalitarian persecution.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 14 August. The student should begin with the following: a short life and selected texts on the Militia of the Immaculata. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
