          "The transition is costly—conflict with the family over property, the choice of a harder life, the acceptance of a spiritual direction that is sometimes harsh. Elizabeth’s second vocation is not comfortable retirement; it is a new availability for the least under a stricter form. The same love that ordered the marriage orders the widowhood under a different set of duties.",
          "Receive changes of state as possible new vocations under God. Prefer Elizabeth’s freer service to pure despair under the loss of the first form of life. Let widowhood still be a path of the Gospel’s availability for those who will take it.",
          "Support widows and others in changed states as people who may have a second call. Prefer the princess’s tertiary path to pure social leftover status. The freer service remains a real vocation.",
          "Widowhood can be a second vocation of freer service after the duties of marriage. Elizabeth of Hungary’s permanent gift is this second path—the princess’s proof that the end of one form of love under God can be the beginning of another, ordered to the same poor and the same Lord."
