    "essay": "St. Elizabeth of Hungary (1207–1231), Princess, Franciscan tertiary, Servant of the poor — Hungarian princess who lived Gospel poverty and care for the sick..\n\nLife. St. Elizabeth of Hungary, 1207–1231, Princess, Franciscan tertiary, Servant of the poor. This figure belongs to the Early Franciscan tertiary movement. Known in the tradition as “Hungarian princess who lived Gospel poverty and care for the sick..” Elizabeth of Hungary, daughter of a king, married the Landgrave of Thuringia and devoted herself to the poor. Widowed young, she lived under Franciscan inspiration and died in 1231. Canonized in 1235.\n\nTeaching. Elizabeth of Hungary, daughter of a king, married the Landgrave of Thuringia and devoted herself to the poor. Widowed young, she lived under Franciscan inspiration and died in 1231. Canonized in 1235.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does royal rank become Franciscan charity among the poor? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Works of mercy; Franciscan tertiary life; Evangelical poverty. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Elizabeth of Hungary is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
