          "Later liturgical development, including Vatican II’s reform, stands in a history that includes this standardization. Reading Pius V requires both gratitude for the unity the books served and openness to the Church’s later judgment about reform. The permanent principle is the liturgy as a common good ordered to the unity of faith—not pure local improvisation as the first liturgical principle.",
          "Hold the liturgy as a common good. Prefer Pius V’s unity of rite under the unity of faith to pure fragmentation of the Latin Church’s prayer. Let the shared books still serve the shared confession.",
