    "essay": "St. Pius V (1504–1572), Dominican, Pope, Implementer of Trent — Dominican pope of the Roman Catechism, reformed Missal, and Lepanto thanksgiving..\n\nLife. SAINT PIUS V, Dominican, pope from 1566 to 1572, 1504–1572, implementer of the Council of Trent. Antonio Ghislieri, a Dominican, became Pope Pius V in 1566. He implemented the Council of Trent with severity and zeal. He died in 1572 and was canonized in 1712.\n\nTeaching. Life. Born Antonio Ghislieri at Bosco, he was a reforming inquisitor and bishop before election. He lived austerely and governed with severity toward heresy and clerical laxity. Canonized 1712.\n\nWritings and teaching. Roman Catechism (1566); Roman Breviary (1568); Roman Missal (1570); support of the Holy League and the feast after Lepanto.\n\nDoctrine. Tridentine consolidation of liturgy, catechesis, and discipline; defense of Catholic Christendom in confessional conflict.\n\nPlace in the Church. Feast: 30 April. The student should begin with the following: the Roman Catechism’s structure as a map of Tridentine doctrine. In the manner of a Catholic encyclopedia article, the present notice orients the reader toward primary sources and the Church’s public memory rather than toward private admiration alone. Dates in the patristic and medieval periods are often approximate; the doctrinal substance received in liturgy and definition is not therefore left uncertain. Where modern critical editions revise attributions or legends, they are to be welcomed, provided the rule of faith remains the measure of interpretation.\n\nHistorical judgment. The Catholic historian neither canonizes every legend nor reduces the saints to social symptoms. Critical study of sources is a duty of truth; skepticism that cannot admit holiness or miracle is a prejudice, not a method. Where the Church has raised a name to the altars, private judgment yields in the order of cult even while historical research continues on secondary details.\n\nDoctrinal reception. Not every opinion of every Father is defined faith. The Church receives persons as saints and doctors according to the whole of their witness, while particular theological theories remain subject to later discrimination—as with Origenist speculation, or Tertullian’s Montanist period, or disputed scholastic opinions. The present articles follow that rule: honor the person, weigh the texts, and hold fast to the creeds and councils.\n\nPlace in the Church. How to study. Read primary sources before secondary summaries. Prefer critical editions where available. Use the liturgy—feasts, collects, readings—as a guide to what the Church publicly values in a life. Compare related figures in the same school or controversy. Finally, measure spiritual claims by the commandments, the creed, and the fruits of charity.",
    "name": "St. Pius V",
    "centralQuestion": "How does a Dominican pope reform liturgy, doctrine, and discipline after Trent?",
