    "essay": "St. Claude de la Colombière (1641–1682), Jesuit priest, Spiritual director of St. Margaret Mary, Apostle of the Sacred Heart — Jesuit director who confirmed and spread devotion to the Sacred Heart..\n\nLife. St. Claude de la Colombière, 1641–1682, Jesuit priest, Spiritual director of St. Margaret Mary, Apostle of the Sacred Heart. This figure belongs to the French Jesuit spirituality · Paray-le-Monial. Known in the tradition as “Jesuit director who confirmed and spread devotion to the Sacred Heart..” Claude de la Colombière was a French Jesuit, preacher, and spiritual director at Paray-le-Monial. He died in 1682 and was canonized in 1992. He is remembered as the apostle of the Sacred Heart beside Margaret Mary.\n\nTeaching. Claude de la Colombière was a French Jesuit, preacher, and spiritual director at Paray-le-Monial. He died in 1682 and was canonized in 1992. He is remembered as the apostle of the Sacred Heart beside Margaret Mary.\n\nTheological focus. The central question associated with this figure may be stated thus: How does Ignatian direction open the Church to the Sacred Heart through trust? Catholic reading situates the answer within the rule of faith, the sacraments, and the life of grace, not within private speculation alone.\n\nPrincipal themes. Sacred Heart; Spiritual direction; Holy confidence. These heads of doctrine should be studied in the primary sources and in the living magisterium rather than in isolation.\n\nFor fuller narrative detail the reader may consult the standard hagiographical and historical notices, always verifying dates and attributions against critical editions and the judgment of the Church.\n\nIn the design of a Catholic encyclopedia, St. Claude de la Colombière is not an ornament of private admiration but a witness ordered to truth. Read the primary texts where available; prefer the consensus of the Fathers and the definitions of the councils where controversy arises; and measure every spiritual claim by charity and the common faith of the Church.\n\nPlace in the Church. Method of reading. The Catholic student approaches such a life with three instruments: the primary texts where they exist; the judgment of the Church in liturgy, canonization, and doctrinal definition; and the sober consensus of historical scholarship. Hagiographical embroidery is neither to be swallowed whole nor sneered at; it is to be weighed. Dates and attributions remain subject to critical revision, but the doctrinal core received by the Church does not float free of these witnesses.",
