    "name": "St. Peter",
    "dates": "c. 30–64/67",
    "epithet": "Prince of the Apostles, first Bishop of Rome",
    "tradition": "early",
    "portrait": "img/figures/pope-peter.jpg",
    "centralQuestion": "What is the Petrine ministry in the life of the Church?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "St. Peter (c. 30–64/67) — Prince of the Apostles, first Bishop of Rome.\n\nLife. St. Peter the Apostle, originally Simon son of Jonah, is the rock upon whom Christ promises to build His Church, the first among the Twelve in the New Testament's own ordering, and the first bishop of Rome in the unbroken tradition of the Catholic Church. The Gospels show him called from the nets, confessing Jesus as the Christ, stumbling in the Passion, restored by the risen Lord, and charged to feed the sheep. Acts presents him as preacher at Pentecost, judge in the early community, and the one through whom the Gentiles are first publicly received without the full yoke of the Mosaic law.\n\nTeaching. Catholic doctrine concerning Peter is not a later political invention laid upon a simple fisherman. It is the Church's reading of Matthew 16, Luke 22, John 21, and the apostolic history under the guidance of the same Spirit who spoke through the apostles. Christ gives Peter a particular responsibility for strengthening his brethren; Peter's authority is exercised through pastoral love—\"Feed my sheep\"—and the Petrine ministry serves the unity of the Church rather than personal prestige.\n\nThe historical Peter is also a man of failure and recovery. His denial is not hidden by the New Testament; his restoration is public. The office that follows is therefore marked from the beginning by mercy as well as authority. Successors in the see of Rome inherit not impeccability of person but a charge of visible unity and a teaching office that the Church has come to recognize, under defined conditions, as capable of definitive judgment in matters of faith and morals.\n\nFor this archive Peter is both figure and office: the apostle in the texts, and the principle of unity that the papacy section unfolds historically. The greatest ideas below stay close to the primary scriptural loci—rock, strengthening the brethren, pastoral feeding, and service of unity—so that later dogmatic development can be seen as organic, not arbitrary.\n\nPlace in the Church. Read the Gospels and Acts first. Then the Church's definitions—from the early appeals to Rome through Pastor Aeternus—appear as the same line drawn out, not a foreign thread sewn on.",
