    "portrait": "img/figures/pope-clement-i.jpg",
    "centralQuestion": "What is the Petrine ministry in the life of the Church?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "St. Clement I (c. 88–97) — Apostolic Father, Bishop of Rome.\n\nLife. St. Clement I of Rome (pope late first century; traditionally d. c. 99) is the earliest successor of Peter for whom we possess a substantial authentic letter. First Clement, written from the church of Rome to the church of Corinth, intervenes in a factional deposition of presbyters and argues for order, humility, and apostolic succession in the ministry.\n\nTeaching. The letter is pastoral before it is juridical. Clement diagnoses jealousy and arrogance, appeals to Old Testament examples of obedience and envy, and presents the apostles as having appointed bishops and deacons with provision for continuity so that the ministry would not become a prize of local politics. He appeals also to the cosmic order—the harmony of the heavens and seasons—as a sign that the Church should not be less ordered than creation. The Roman church speaks with authority and persuasion to another apostolic church—an early sign of Rome’s care for the peace of the wider body. A hymn to love near the end echoes Paul: without charity, hierarchy and charism both fail.\n\nPlace in the Church. In this archive Clement is the sub-apostolic witness of ordered ministry and Roman solicitude. Catholic ecclesiology of holy orders and succession reads 1 Clement as a primary document of the age immediately after the apostles. Read chapters 42–44 on succession and 49–50 on love. The Catholic pattern is already visible: succession and charity together—order without love becomes domination; equality without order becomes chaos. Every later fight over appointments can still hear the letter’s demand: do not tear the body for the sake of ambition.",
