          "Gregory VII understands the papal office as a responsibility for the whole Church that includes the duty to correct and, when required, to confront—even emperors. Catholic theology of primacy’s corrective dimension finds a dramatic medieval enactment here. The pope is not only a symbol of unity; he is a judge under the law of God for the sake of the flock. When Henry IV is excommunicated and declared deposed, the act is ordered to the liberty of the Church and the correction of a ruler who would own the spiritual power. The confrontation is the office’s burden, not its pleasure.",
          "Later Catholic development of the relation between spiritual and temporal power revises the medieval instruments. The permanent core is the duty to correct for the faith and the liberty of the Church. A primacy that never confronts when the Gospel requires it has left Gregory’s sense of responsibility even if it keeps the title.",
