    "tradition": "medieval",
    "portrait": "img/figures/pope-gregory-vii.jpg",
    "centralQuestion": "What is the Petrine ministry in the life of the Church?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "St. Gregory VII (1073–1085) — Gregorian Reform, Hildebrand.\n\nLife. St. Gregory VII (Hildebrand, c. 1015–1085; pope 1073–1085) is the central figure of the Gregorian Reform—the eleventh-century struggle to free the Church from lay investiture, simony, and the reduction of the clergy to a branch of royal administration. A monk of severe seriousness, he brought to the papacy the conviction that spiritual authority must not be a department of the state.\n\nTeaching. His conflict with Emperor Henry IV, including Canossa and the later bitter phases of the Investiture Controversy, dramatized the claim of the libertas ecclesiae. Reform of clerical celibacy and the attack on the sale of offices aimed at the credibility of the priesthood: a bought office and a scandalous life preach louder than any sermon. The Dictatus Papae and his register of letters show a pope who believed the Roman see must actively correct and, when required, confront. Personal austerity underwrote public reform; a soft curia cannot credibly demand hard purity of the parish clergy. He died in exile with the words that he had loved justice and hated iniquity—and therefore died in exile.\n\nPlace in the Church. Catholic doctrine of the Church’s freedom in spiritualibus stands in permanent debt to this struggle, whatever historians debate about tactics and tone. In this archive Gregory is the reformer-pope who made the independence of the altar a non-negotiable. Read the letters of the conflict and the reform canons. Clean the spring of clerical life; refuse to sell the keys to the throne. When states claim the altar, Gregory’s no remains in the Church’s memory."
