          "The Fourth Lateran Council under Innocent III joins doctrinal definition and disciplinary reform in one body of canons—the Church teaches and orders her life together. Catholic conciliar practice finds a classical medieval instance here. The confession of faith, the doctrine of the sacraments (including the word transubstantiatio), the reform of clergy, the care of the sacraments, and the structures of Church life are not separated into pure theory and pure administration. A council is the Church’s public act of believing and ordering under the same authority.",
          "Lateran IV’s doctrinal yield includes a clear profession of the eucharistic change and of the trinitarian and christological faith. Its disciplinary yield includes annual confession and communion, reform of clerical life, and measures for the Church’s pastoral care. The join is the point: doctrine without discipline remains abstract; discipline without doctrine becomes pure management.",
          "Hold doctrine and discipline together in the Church’s public acts. Prefer Lateran IV’s integration to pure doctrinal statements without reform and pure managerial reforms without faith. Let the council still model the Church’s double work under one authority.",
          "When the Church teaches, let her also order her life. When she orders her life, let her teach. Prefer Innocent’s conciliar integration to every split that leaves faith and practice in separate compartments. The same council remains the measure.",
          "Doctrine and discipline belong in the same council: the Church teaches and orders her life together. Innocent III’s permanent conciliar gift is this integration—the Fourth Lateran’s proof that the Church’s public faith and the Church’s public order are one act under the same Lord."
