    "name": "Bl. Pius IX",
    "dates": "1846–1878",
    "epithet": "Immaculate Conception, Vatican I",
    "tradition": "catholic",
    "portrait": "img/figures/pope-pius-ix.jpg",
    "centralQuestion": "What is the Petrine ministry in the life of the Church?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "Bl. Pius IX (1846–1878) — Immaculate Conception, Vatican I.\n\nLife. Bl. Pius IX (Giovanni Maria Mastai-Ferretti, 1792–1878; pope 1846–1878) reigned longer than any other pope in history and presided over the definition of the Immaculate Conception, the First Vatican Council’s teaching on primacy and infallibility, and the loss of the Papal States. His pontificate moved from early reformist hopes to a long conflict with liberal and nationalist reconfigurations of Europe.\n\nTeaching. Ineffabilis Deus (1854) defined that Mary was preserved from original sin from the first moment of her conception by a singular grace in view of Christ’s merits—a Christological dogma of preservative redemption. Pastor Aeternus (1870) at Vatican I defined papal primacy and the infallible magisterium of the Roman Pontiff under strict conditions when teaching ex cathedra a doctrine of faith or morals to be held by the whole Church—often caricatured, carefully limited in the text itself. The Syllabus of Errors and the “prisoner of the Vatican” situation after 1870 marked the papacy’s confrontation with modern political claims; later Catholic social teaching would refine engagement with modern freedoms while keeping the refusal of erastian absorption. The loss of temporal sovereignty became, in providence, a paradoxical liberation for global spiritual mission.\n\nPlace in the Church. Beatified in 2000, Pius IX remains a polarizing historical memory and a doctrinal milestone. In this archive he is the pope of Mary Immaculate and of Vatican I’s Petrine definitions. Read Ineffabilis Deus and Pastor Aeternus. The office is for the Church’s unity in truth; the man’s long suffering under a hostile age is part of the modern papacy’s identity before the Lateran settlement."
