        "idea": "The social question is a theological question: capital, labor, and the state stand under the Gospel and natural law.",
        "source": "Rerum Novarum",
        "article": [
          "When Leo XIII published Rerum Novarum in 1891, the industrial world had already produced a new class of misery that neither pure market liberalism nor revolutionary socialism seemed able to name correctly. Factories had gathered men and women into conditions that treated labor as a cost to be minimized and the worker as a replaceable part. Against the socialist proposal to abolish private property and against the liberal refusal to let the state or the Church interfere with contracts, Leo insisted that the condition of the working classes is a question that belongs to theology and to the natural law as much as to economics. Capital and labor are not neutral forces; they are activities of persons who stand under the Gospel. The encyclical therefore opens a permanent Catholic claim: the social question cannot be left to ideologues of left or right alone.",
          "The doctrine of Rerum Novarum is carefully balanced. Private property is legitimate and rooted in the nature of the human person who must provide for family and future; it is not an absolute that cancels the universal destination of goods or the claims of the desperate. The worker has a right to a wage sufficient for frugal comfort, to rest, to association in guilds or unions that are not engines of class hatred, and to the protection of the state when weaker parties cannot protect themselves. The state has duties toward the common good without absorbing the whole of social life into its administration. Leo refuses both the night-watchman state that ignores the poor and the total state that becomes the only parent of the citizen. The family remains prior; intermediate bodies remain real; the Church retains her right to teach moral truth about economic life.",
          "Reception of the encyclical created the modern tradition of Catholic social teaching. Later documents—Quadragesimo Anno, Mater et Magistra, Laborem Exercens, Centesimus Annus—develop rather than cancel its principles. Thomistic language of justice, the common good, and the dignity of the person becomes the ordinary grammar with which the magisterium addresses wages, unions, unemployment, and the preferential care of the poor. Critics from pure free-market schools and from Marxist schools have both found Leo insufficiently radical; the Church has judged that insufficiency to be fidelity to the person who is neither a pure individual economic actor nor a pure specimen of a class.",
          "Pastoral application remains concrete. Parishes and dioceses that never speak about wages, working conditions, or the family under economic pressure have not yet heard Rerum Novarum as a living document. Employers who are Catholic are addressed as men and women under judgment, not only as donors. Workers are addressed as persons with duties of diligence and with rights that do not depend on the goodwill of the powerful. The preferential option for the poor in later teaching is already present in Leo’s refusal to treat the misery of the industrial worker as a private misfortune.",
          "Read Rerum Novarum still. Apply its principles to precarious labor, concentrated capital, and the digital reorganization of work. Treat the social question as theological: every contract, every wage, and every policy that touches the person who works is already under the Gospel and the natural law. Leo’s charter remains the starting point of every serious Catholic economics—and a permanent examination of conscience for every economy that still asks what work is for and whom it is for."
        ],
