    "essay": "Leo XIII (1878–1903) — Rerum Novarum, Thomistic revival.\n\nLife. Leo XIII (Vincenzo Gioacchino Pecci, 1810–1903; pope 1878–1903) opened the modern era of the papacy’s sustained engagement with industrial society, philosophical reconstruction, and a global Catholic culture after the loss of the Papal States. His long pontificate produced a body of teaching that still structures Catholic social thought, seminary intellectual life, and popular devotion.\n\nTeaching. Rerum Novarum (1891) addressed the condition of the working classes: the dignity of labor, the right of association, the limits of both socialist collectivism and liberal individualism, and the moral duties of the state under the common good. Aeterni Patris (1879) promoted the restoration of Thomistic philosophy as a common grammar for Catholic schools against fragmented modern systems. Repeated encyclicals on the Rosary treated Marian meditation on the mysteries as a mass form of Gospel contemplation, refusing the contempt of elites for popular piety. Political encyclicals such as Immortale Dei and Diuturnum developed a doctrine of legitimate authority and the Church’s liberty in her proper sphere—against both erastian absorption and revolutionary denial of authority.\n\nPlace in the Church. Leo’s papacy thus joins social doctrine, intellectual policy, and devotion under one leonine confidence: the Church has something to say to the modern world without becoming a department of any party. In this archive he is the hinge between the nineteenth-century “prisoner of the Vatican” situation and the twentieth-century global magisterium. Read Rerum Novarum and Aeterni Patris as twin charters—justice for the worker, wisdom for the schools. Give Caesar what is Caesar’s; refuse to give him the keys.",
