    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "St. Pius X (1903–1914) — Restore all things in Christ.\n\nLife. St. Pius X (Giuseppe Sarto, 1835–1914; pope 1903–1914) took as his motto Instaurare omnia in Christo—to restore all things in Christ—and applied it to catechesis, the sacraments, sacred music, and the defense of doctrine against modernism. A pastor of modest origin, he brought to the papacy a preference for clear teaching and Eucharistic accessibility under due disposition.\n\nTeaching. His decrees on frequent and early Communion changed Western pastoral practice: the Host is food for the journey, not a prize reserved only for the spiritually finished. Catechetical reform treated the ignorance of the young as a pastoral emergency—the ordinary insurance against a faithless generation. The motu proprio on sacred music sought dignity and prayerfulness in worship against theatrical excess. Pascendi Dominici Gregis and Lamentabili diagnosed modernism as a cluster of errors that relocate revelation into evolving religious experience and empty dogma of stable content—documents still debated for tone, permanent in their concern for the fixed deposit of faith.\n\nPlace in the Church. Pius X was canonized in 1954. In this archive he represents a reforming papacy of doctrinal firmness and sacramental generosity. Read the Communion decrees and the catechetical program as the positive face of the same pontificate that issued Pascendi. Restore all things in Christ means both protecting the creed and feeding the flock. A Church that cannot teach its children will not keep its adults.",
