    "essay": "St. John XXIII (1958–1963) — Convoked Vatican II.\n\nLife. St. John XXIII (Angelo Giuseppe Roncalli, 1881–1963; pope 1958–1963) convened the Second Vatican Council and set its early tone with a call to discern the signs of the times and to offer the medicine of mercy. A career diplomat and pastor of Venice before his election, he brought to the papacy a paternal accessibility that earned him the enduring name “Good Pope John.”\n\nTeaching. His opening address to the council, Gaudet Mater Ecclesia, rejected the grimness of those who see only ruin in the modern age and asked for a teaching that would present the faith whole, with pastoral charity rather than with severity alone. Pacem in Terris (1963), addressed to all people of good will, developed rights, duties, and peace among nations in the nuclear age—a global voice of Catholic social teaching. The Journal of a Soul reveals the interior life behind the public smile—simple, serious, formed long before the tiara. He died before the council completed its work; Paul VI would steer its conclusion and implementation.\n\nPlace in the Church. Catholic interpretation of Vatican II still turns partly on John XXIII’s intention: renewal without rupture, aggiornamento under the deposit of faith. In this archive he is the pope who opened the windows and insisted the foundations remain. Read Pacem in Terris and the council’s opening address. Mercy and truth are not rivals; diagnosis of the age is for the sake of the Gospel’s prescription, not for fashion. Open the windows; do not move the foundations.",
