          "Paul VI oversaw the reform of the Roman liturgy after Sacrosanctum Concilium, seeking the active participation of the faithful in the one sacrifice of Christ. Vernacular language, simplified rites, and a clearer ordering of the celebration were ordered to that end: that the people might understand, offer, and be formed by the paschal mystery made present on the altar. The stated aim was evangelical and pastoral, not archaeological fashion or experimental theatre. Novelty for its own sake was never the criterion he named.",
          "The implementation produced both genuine renewal and serious wounds. Where the reform recovered Scripture, preaching, and conscious participation in the sacrifice, it bore fruit. Where it became a permission structure for banality, loss of the sacred, or rupture with the Church’s musical and ritual memory, it failed its own purpose. Paul VI authorized the new Missal and also lamented abuses. The measure remains the worthy celebration of Calvary made present, not the preference of a party.",
          "Catholic debate over the liturgical reform continues decades later—sometimes as a debate about texts and rubrics, sometimes as a proxy for deeper conflicts about the council and modernity. A hermeneutic of continuity asks whether participation in the one sacrifice has been deepened or whether the rite has been made a mirror of the assembly. Paul VI’s intention stands as a criterion even when particular applications are judged wanting.",
          "Pastoral responsibility now includes the reform of the reform where needed: recovery of silence, sacred music, orientation toward the Lord, and fidelity to the liturgical books. It also includes gratitude for what was genuinely gained in the people’s access to the Word and the clearer shape of the celebration.",
          "Serve the people’s true participation in the one sacrifice. Refuse novelty as entertainment. Correct what obscures the altar’s meaning. Paul VI’s liturgical aim remains the right criterion—whether every implementation has met it is a question for continuous, humble reform under the same Lord who offers himself in every valid Mass."
