    "name": "St. Paul VI",
    "dates": "1963–1978",
    "epithet": "Humanae Vitae, closed Vatican II",
    "tradition": "catholic",
    "portrait": "img/figures/pope-paul-vi.jpg",
    "centralQuestion": "What is the Petrine ministry in the life of the Church?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "St. Paul VI (1963–1978) — Humanae Vitae, closed Vatican II.\n\nLife. St. Paul VI (Giovanni Battista Montini, 1897–1978; pope 1963–1978) concluded the Second Vatican Council and bore the burden of its first implementation through a decade of cultural upheaval. A close collaborator of Pius XII and a man of refined culture, he sought to hold reform and continuity together when parties on left and right claimed the council against each other.\n\nTeaching. Ecclesiam Suam (1964) proposed dialogue as a method of mission without replacing proclamation. Populorum Progressio (1967) developed the social doctrine of development and the peoples. Humanae Vitae (1968) reaffirmed the moral teaching on the transmission of life and the inseparable connection of the unitive and procreative meanings of the conjugal act—the most contested document of his reign, and a test of whether the magisterium may bind conscience against cultural consensus. He predicted social consequences of separating love from openness to life. He oversaw the reform of the Roman liturgy according to Sacrosanctum Concilium, aiming at active participation in the one sacrifice; debates over fruits and abuses continue, while his stated end remained evangelical. His travels—including the Holy Land and the United Nations—modeled a global papacy.\n\nPlace in the Church. Paul VI was canonized in 2018. In this archive he is the pope of the council’s aftermath: fidelity under turbulence. Read Humanae Vitae and Evangelii Nuntiandi for the moral and missionary poles of his mind. Reform the house; do not burn it to warm a faction. Cross oceans; carry the same creed."
