    "essay": "St. John Paul II (1978–2005) — Totus Tuus, Catechism.\n\nLife. St. John Paul II (Karol Wojtyła, 1920–2005; pope 1978–2005) exercised the primacy on a global stage for more than twenty-six years, joining a personalist philosophy forged under totalitarian pressure, a dramatic sense of history, and intense Marian devotion to the defense of the person, the family, and the freedom of the Church.\n\nTeaching. Redemptor Hominis (1979) set the program: Christ reveals man to himself; the person is the way of the Church. The Theology of the Body catecheses reread sexuality as a language of total gift ordered to love and life. Laborem Exercens deepened the spirituality and ethics of work against alienation. Fides et Ratio defended the partnership of faith and reason against nihilism and fideism. Evangelium Vitae confronted the culture of death. His role in the Polish crisis and in wider human-rights discourse made the papacy a player in the century’s political imagination without reducing the office to politics. The Catechism of the Catholic Church (1992) under his authority offered a comprehensive post-conciliar synthesis of doctrine. Dives in Misericordia placed divine mercy at the center of the Church’s response to the twentieth century’s wounds.\n\nPlace in the Church. Canonized in 2014, he remains a polarizing memory in some debates and a clear intercessor in popular devotion. In this archive he is the personalist and missionary pope of the century’s end. Read Redemptor Hominis and Fides et Ratio. Measure programs by the person under God; measure cultures by whether they can still say that truth and love are real.",
