    "essay": "Benedict XVI (2005–2013) — Co-worker of the truth.\n\nLife. Benedict XVI (Joseph Ratzinger, 1927–2022; pope 2005–2013) brought to the chair of Peter one of the strongest theological minds of the modern era—a peritus at Vatican II, long-time prefect of the Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith, and a writer of unusual clarity on liturgy, Christology, and the crisis of truth in the West. His resignation in 2013 was itself a historical act of the office’s self-understanding.\n\nTeaching. Deus Caritas Est located Christianity in the encounter with a Person, not first in a moral system or social program. Sacramentum Caritatis and his earlier Spirit of the Liturgy defended worship as received gift ordered to adoration against self-celebration of the assembly. The Regensburg address argued for the partnership of biblical faith and rationality against religious violence and against a reason closed to transcendence. He proposed a hermeneutic of reform in continuity for interpreting Vatican II against narratives of rupture that imagine a different religion before and after the council. The phrase “dictatorship of relativism” named a culture that treats binding moral and religious truth as the only forbidden claim.\n\nPlace in the Church. As pope emeritus he lived in prayer near the successors he had served. In this archive Benedict is the doctor-pope of logos and liturgy. Read Deus Caritas Est, the Regensburg text, and The Spirit of the Liturgy. Christianity begins with meeting Christ; the Church defends the mind’s capacity for truth because that truth has a face. Hold the council with the fathers, not against the fathers.",
