    "portrait": "img/figures/pope-francis.jpg",
    "centralQuestion": "How is the Petrine ministry exercised for mercy and unity in the present age?",
    "centralQuestionLabel": "His central question",
    "essay": "Francis (2013–) — Bishop of Rome · Evangelii Gaudium.\n\nLife. Pope Francis (Jorge Mario Bergoglio, b. 1936), elected in 2013 as the first Jesuit pope and the first from the Americas, has shaped the contemporary exercise of the primacy around the themes of mercy, missionary discipleship, care for the poor, and care for our common home. Formed in the Society of Jesus and in the pastoral realities of Buenos Aires, he brought to the see of Peter a preference for proximity to the wounded—the “field hospital” Church—joined to a sustained critique of clerical self-reference and throwaway culture.\n\nTeaching. His major teaching documents map that program. Evangelii Gaudium (2013) calls for a missionary transformation of the Church’s life and structures. Laudato Si’ (2015) places ecology inside Catholic social doctrine as the cry of the earth and the cry of the poor together. Amoris Laetitia (2016) addresses marriage and family with an accent on accompaniment and the complexity of concrete situations, sparking ongoing debate about the relation of pastoral practice to moral norms. Fratelli Tutti (2020) develops social friendship and political charity against a background of fragmentation and violence. Synodal processes have been promoted as a style of listening across the communion, under the bishops and the pope—not, in his stated intention, as a parliament replacing the magisterium.\n\nCatholic reception of Francis is polarized in many regions: some hear the Gospel’s preference for the lost sheep and the periphery; others fear ambiguity on settled points of moral teaching. A just reading holds the distinction between tone, prudential emphasis, and the deposit of faith he is bound to confirm. Mercy that never names sin is incomplete; law that never approaches the wounded is incomplete. His pontificate forces the perennial Catholic pair into public view.\n\nPlace in the Church. In this archive Francis is the reigning-era witness of a papacy that seeks to walk with a global flock under modern conditions while still confessing the creed of Nicaea and Chalcedon. Measure him, as every pope, by continuity with the apostolic faith and by the actual fruits of charity and clarity in the churches.",
