        "article": [
          "Alphonsus’s Glories of Mary ranks Marian devotion as the appointed channel of confidence for the people of God—service of the Redeemer, not a rival center. Catholic Mariology finds in him a popular and theological defense of the Mother’s role under Christ. The book’s rhetoric is warm and maximal in places; its doctrinal intent is Christocentric: Mary exists for Jesus, and confidence in her is confidence in the economy God actually established. When Marian devotion is treated as pure optional decoration, Alphonsus’s urgency corrects it. When Marian devotion is treated as pure independent salvation, the same Christocentric frame corrects that too.",
          "The pastoral context is the mission to people who need a face of mercy they can approach without pure terror. Alphonsus does not invent Mary’s role; he amplifies the tradition’s trust in the Mother of the Redeemer for souls who struggle. The Glories remains a classic of that trust under the Church’s later and more precise Marian definitions.",
          "Practice Marian confidence under Christ the Redeemer. Prefer Alphonsus’s channel of trust to pure neglect of Mary and pure rivalry with Christ. Let the Mother still serve the Son’s mercy in the people’s prayer under the Church’s faith.",
          "When Marian devotion is attacked or exaggerated, prefer the Redemptorist’s Christocentric intent. Prefer the Glories’ pastoral trust to every spirituality that only knows pure minimalism or pure independence of Mary from Jesus. The channel remains; the Redeemer remains the only Savior under God.",
          "Mary is the appointed channel of confidence for the people of God—devotion to her serves the Redeemer, not a rival center. Alphonsus’s permanent Marian gift is this ordered confidence—the Doctor’s proof that the people’s love of Mary can be a school of trust in Jesus, under a theology that never needed to choose between the Mother and the Son."
        ],
        "quotes": []
