    "essay": "Cardinal Cajetan (1469–1534), Cardinal, Theologian, Thomist, Biblical commentator — Tommaso de Vio · Commentator on the Summa.\n\nLife. Cardinal Cajetan (Tommaso de Vio, 1469–1534) is the Renaissance Dominican whose commentary on the Summa Theologiae shaped the way centuries of students read St. Thomas. Master of the sacred palace, general of his order, and cardinal, he joined scholastic precision to the new demands of print, reform, and controversy with Luther. Tommaso de Vio, known as Cajetan from his birthplace Gaeta, was a Dominican cardinal and the most influential Renaissance commentator on the Summa Theologiae. He met Luther at Augsburg in 1518 and wrote extensively on Scripture, the papacy, and the philosophical questions of his day.\n\nTeaching. At Augsburg in 1518 he examined Luther on behalf of the Holy See. The meeting did not heal the breach; it clarified how far the new teaching on justification and authority had already moved from the Catholic synthesis. Cajetan’s later writings on the papacy and on Scripture defend the visible Church’s structure without abandoning the scholastic tools of distinction and definition.\n\nPlace in the Church. As a commentator, Cajetan is not a pure repeater. He settles questions Thomas left open, engages Scotist and humanist objections, and sometimes advances positions later Thomists would qualify—yet the overall effect is to make the Summa a living textbook for the Catholic Reformation rather than a medieval monument. For analogy of being, the structure of the sacraments, and the analysis of faith and justice, his pages remain a standard reference. The student who wants Thomas as the tradition actually received him in the sixteenth century still goes through Cajetan.",
