          "Read within Catholic dogma, this is not a denial of judgment or hell, but a summons to share the patience of the Father who desires salvation.",
          "Pastoral life needs this accent: rigor without mercy becomes cruelty; mercy without truth becomes flattery.",
          "Isaac’s doctrine of mercy flows from the character of God. The ascetic path that hardens the heart against the weak has misunderstood the goal. Compassion is not sentimentalism; it is participation in the patience of the Father who desires the salvation of the sinner. Read within Catholic dogma, this accent never cancels judgment, hell, or the need for repentance; it refuses to make cruelty a proof of orthodoxy.",
          "The Syriac tradition’s warmth on this point has sometimes been misread as doctrinal laxity. The Catholic rule is the same as with any Father: receive what accords with the apostolic faith; measure private intensity by the creed and the commandments. Isaac at his best pushes the monk toward a love that suffers with the world without baptizing the world’s sins.",
          "Pastoral life needs both edges—truth and mercy—held together. Isaac is a classical witness for the edge of mercy that modern rigorism and modern permissiveness both miss in different ways."
